Title:
Writing extensions in Sliver C2

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post explains how to extend Sliver C2 functionality using Sliver “aliases” and “extensions,” and why extensions are often preferable for OPSEC.  
- It contrasts aliases (wrappers around `execute-assembly`/`sideload` that spawn a sacrificial process and use Donut to run .NET) with extensions (native shared libraries reflectively loaded into the existing implant process).  
- The article walks through the on-disk structure and manifest formats (`alias.json` vs `extension.json`), and highlights key fields like `command_name`, `entrypoint`, and `files`.  
- It details the Sliver extension API: an exported `entrypoint(argsBuffer, bufferSize, goCallback)` function and a single-use callback to return output to the server/client.  
- A concrete example builds a Windows DLL extension that reads clipboard contents via Win32 APIs and returns the data through the callback, then shows install/load/run flows inside the Sliver client.  
- Useful for red teamers and tool developers who want custom in-memory capabilities without adding bulk to implants or spawning extra processes.

Technical Focus:
- Sliver C2 alias vs extension execution models
- Reflective DLL loading inside implant process memory
- Donut-based .NET assembly-to-shellcode execution (aliases)
- Extension manifests (`extension.json`) and command registration
- Sliver extension ABI: exported entrypoint + Go callback bridge
- Windows API usage in native modules (clipboard example)

Use Cases:
- Build custom Sliver post-exploitation modules that run in-process for better OPSEC
- Package and distribute team-specific tooling via Sliver extensions/Armory-style workflows
- Implement long-running implant-resident capabilities (e.g., monitors, collectors) instead of one-shot execute-assembly
- Rapidly integrate native Windows functionality (Win32) into Sliver operations

Keywords:
Sliver C2, extensions, aliases, reflective loading, Reflective DLL Injection, Donut, execute-assembly, sideload, process injection, sacrificial process, in-memory execution, extension.json, alias.json, goCallback, entrypoint export, MinGW-w64, x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc, Windows API, OpenClipboard, GetClipboardData, CF_TEXT, CLR, AppDomain, RPC RegisterExtension, CallExtension, OPSEC